<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Its ONLY consumer is HELPER_ENTITLEMENTS in meta/signMachOResources.ts, which
  is why it lives here in meta/ rather than in src-tauri/.

  This is deliberately NOT src-tauri/entitlements.plist. That is the
  app/sidecar file, carrying the JIT entitlement, and both the code comment
  at meta/signMachOResources.ts:250-257 and the notarization todo
  (.planning/todos/pending/2026-09-17-notarization-rejects-253-unsigned-binaries-under-contents-resources.md)
  forbid it ever reaching a helper; passing it here would be a silent grant
  of JIT to 253 binaries not shown to need it.

  The measured reason for this grant: signed with the Developer ID identity
  under the hardened runtime and no entitlements, steam-bridge-helper dies at
  dlopen before reaching SteamAPI_Init with "code signature ... not valid for
  use in process: mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have
  different Team IDs", because it loads Valve's libsteam_api.dylib out of the
  user's installed Steam: third-party, outside the 253, never our Team ID.

  This key is granted to steam-bridge-helper ALONE and must never be applied
  to the other 252.

  NOTE TO ANY EDITOR: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen. Writing
  one here is not a style slip; codesign rejects the whole file with
  "Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error", the
  helper then ships unentitled, and the dlopen crash above returns. This
  happened once, on 2026-09-23, and the jest suite did NOT catch it because
  the plist npm package parses what AMFI refuses.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
